{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-28273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-1+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $4$, find the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4593e39bbb6153174c5da5d7c01a37b167851ce0d7fdd3c078f62b6a4d04cbd"} {"question" "q-28273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-1+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(-1+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}))(x-(-1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(-1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})-x(-1+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})+(-1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})(-1+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x+\\sqrt{3}x\\text{i}+x-\\sqrt{3}x\\text{i}+1-3{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+3 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=-1\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $4.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}+2x+4) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+8x+16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4593e39bbb6153174c5da5d7c01a37b167851ce0d7fdd3c078f62b6a4d04cbd"} {"question" "q-28273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8682edcd46e54673e901be199509870889901141c7b9c70aa5f02d4663d388d8"} {"question" "q-28273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4593e39bbb6153174c5da5d7c01a37b167851ce0d7fdd3c078f62b6a4d04cbd"} {"question" "q-28273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4{x}^{2}+16x-8=0"] ["math" "4{x}^{2}+8x+16=0"] ["math" "4{x}^{2}-16x-8=0"] ["math" "4{x}^{2}-2x+4=0"] ["math" "4{x}^{2}-2x-16=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4593e39bbb6153174c5da5d7c01a37b167851ce0d7fdd3c078f62b6a4d04cbd"} {"question" "q-28273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4{x}^{2}+8x+16=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4593e39bbb6153174c5da5d7c01a37b167851ce0d7fdd3c078f62b6a4d04cbd"} {"question" "q-274997" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-\\frac{2\\text{i}}{3}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $-27,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4593e39bbb6153174c5da5d7c01a37b167851ce0d7fdd3c078f62b6a4d04cbd"} {"question" "q-274997" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-\\frac{2\\text{i}}{3}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=\\frac{2\\text{i}}{3}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{2\\text{i}}{3})(x+\\frac{2\\text{i}}{3})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\frac{2\\text{i}}{3})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{9}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{2\\text{i}}{3},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $-27.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $-27.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-27({x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}) & =0 \\\\ -27{x}^{2}-12 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4593e39bbb6153174c5da5d7c01a37b167851ce0d7fdd3c078f62b6a4d04cbd"} {"question" "q-274997" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8682edcd46e54673e901be199509870889901141c7b9c70aa5f02d4663d388d8"} {"question" "q-274997" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4593e39bbb6153174c5da5d7c01a37b167851ce0d7fdd3c078f62b6a4d04cbd"} {"question" "q-274997" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-27x^2-12"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4593e39bbb6153174c5da5d7c01a37b167851ce0d7fdd3c078f62b6a4d04cbd"} {"question" "q-281311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=8\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $1,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4593e39bbb6153174c5da5d7c01a37b167851ce0d7fdd3c078f62b6a4d04cbd"} {"question" "q-281311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=8\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-8\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-8\\text{i})(x+8\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(8\\text{i})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-64(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+64 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4593e39bbb6153174c5da5d7c01a37b167851ce0d7fdd3c078f62b6a4d04cbd"} {"question" "q-281311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8682edcd46e54673e901be199509870889901141c7b9c70aa5f02d4663d388d8"} {"question" "q-281311" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4593e39bbb6153174c5da5d7c01a37b167851ce0d7fdd3c078f62b6a4d04cbd"} {"question" "q-281311" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x^2+64"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4593e39bbb6153174c5da5d7c01a37b167851ce0d7fdd3c078f62b6a4d04cbd"} {"question" "q-281333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $16,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4593e39bbb6153174c5da5d7c01a37b167851ce0d7fdd3c078f62b6a4d04cbd"} {"question" "q-281333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4})(x+\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{6}{16}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{8} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $16.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $16.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16({x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{8}) & =0 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}+6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4593e39bbb6153174c5da5d7c01a37b167851ce0d7fdd3c078f62b6a4d04cbd"} {"question" "q-281333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8682edcd46e54673e901be199509870889901141c7b9c70aa5f02d4663d388d8"} {"question" "q-281333" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4593e39bbb6153174c5da5d7c01a37b167851ce0d7fdd3c078f62b6a4d04cbd"} {"question" "q-281333" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16x^2+6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4593e39bbb6153174c5da5d7c01a37b167851ce0d7fdd3c078f62b6a4d04cbd"} {"question" "q-281348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a complex root $x=-2+\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $1,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4593e39bbb6153174c5da5d7c01a37b167851ce0d7fdd3c078f62b6a4d04cbd"} {"question" "q-281348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-2+\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}$ is a root, we know that its complex conjugate $x=-2-\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}$ is also a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(-2+\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}))(x-(-2-\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(-2+\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i})-x(-2-\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i})+(-2+\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i})(-2-\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-\\cancel{x\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}}+2x+\\cancel{x\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}}+4-7{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+4+7 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+11 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4593e39bbb6153174c5da5d7c01a37b167851ce0d7fdd3c078f62b6a4d04cbd"} {"question" "q-281348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8682edcd46e54673e901be199509870889901141c7b9c70aa5f02d4663d388d8"} {"question" "q-281348" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4593e39bbb6153174c5da5d7c01a37b167851ce0d7fdd3c078f62b6a4d04cbd"} {"question" "q-281348" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x^2+4x+11"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987352/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4593e39bbb6153174c5da5d7c01a37b167851ce0d7fdd3c078f62b6a4d04cbd"}] "usage" {} "basis" 970 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
